{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "i recently watched the movie \"the social network\" and here is my detailed review in all lower case as requested:\n\n**movie title:** the social network\n**director:** david fincher\n**release year:** 2010\n**genre:** biographical drama\n\n**summary:**\nthe movie tells the story of how facebook came into existence, focusing on the life of mark zuckerberg, a harvard university student. the film explores the creation of the social networking site, the legal battles that followed, and the personal relationships that were affected in the process.\n\n**acting:**\n- jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg gives a compelling performance, portraying the character's intelligence, arrogance, and vulnerability.\n- andrew garfield as eduard sazo, mark's friend and initial facebook co-founder, brings depth to the film with his portrayal of betrayal and friendship.\n- justin timberlake as sean parker, co-founder of napster, adds a layer of charm and strategic brilliance to the movie.\n\n**direction and cinematography:**\ndavid fincher's direction is impeccable, weaving together multiple timelines seamlessly. the cinematography is dark and muted, reflecting the mood and atmosphere of the characters' emotional states and the competitive academic environment.\n\n**script:**\naaron sorkin's screenplay is sharp, witty, and fast-paced. the dialogue is engaging and accurately reflects the tech and legal jargon of the time, making the complex subject matter accessible.\n\n**music:**\nthe score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and perfectly complements the on-screen tension and emotional depth.\n\n**critique:**\n- some characters feel slightly one-dimensional, particularly the women in mark's life.\n- the pacing, while generally good, slows down slightly in the middle act.\n\n**rating:** 4.5/5\n\n**recommendation:**\nthe social network is a must-watch fo